  "title": "Don't let a Markdown link's text stand in for its destination",
  "file": "src/integrations/issue-queue.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A trusted maintainer writes a slice line as a Markdown link whose visible text says #3 but whose address points at issue 3 in a different repository. The queue reads only the visible text, treats the line as naming this epic's own sub-issue #3, and wrongly reports the epic as ready for acceptance, or fails to report an unfiled slice.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/f1.XXXX/f1.ts  (throwaway script calling epicAttention at head: local closed child #2 and open, approved child #3; body variants below)",
    "output": "foreign acceptance link: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}   <- body '- Build: #2 / - Acceptance: [#3](https://github.com/other/repo/issues/3)'\nforeign build link: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}   <- '- Build: [#2](https://github.com/other/repo/issues/2)' hides the unfiled line\ncontrol no ref: {\"state\":\"unfiled\",...}"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, src/integrations/issue-queue.ts:113-116: `const refs = (text) => [...text.matchAll(/(?<![\\w/])#([1-9][0-9]*)\\b/g)].map((m) => issueKey(repo, Number(m[1]))).filter((k) => childKeys.has(k));`. The `#3` inside `[#3]` is preceded by `[`, so it matches and is keyed to the epic's own repository. The link's destination is never looked at. Only bodies from trusted authors are parsed, which is why this needs an unusual, mislabelled link."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An epic author links a planned slice to another repository using a number that also exists among the epic\u2019s children. The queue mistakes the link for that local child, suppressing the missing-slice warning and potentially reporting that only acceptance remains.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/epic-adjudication-qvwkp3i5/f1.ts\"",
    "output": "With closed local child #2 and approved open local child #3:\nbare local acceptance: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}\nforeign acceptance link: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}\nforeign build link: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}\nnonissue link: {\"state\":\"ready_for_acceptance\",\"items\":[\"#3\"]}\nunfiled control: {\"state\":\"unfiled\",\"items\":[\"- Build: no child reference\"]}"
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD extracts references using text.matchAll(/(?<![\\w/])#([1-9][0-9]*)\\b/g), then assigns each match issueKey(repo, Number(m[1])). This reads Markdown labels without considering their destinations. Both unfiled detection and acceptance identification use these references. The base has no epicAttention implementation, so this defect is introduced. After completing the HEAD probes, the same inputs against the later implementation returned unfiled for foreign and non-issue destinations while preserving the bare-reference control."
  }
